@title CI Provider Comparison for Firmware and Cloud
@subtitle Developer Experience at Ridgeback Robotics, replacing Jenkins

We run about 38,000 build minutes per month across 120 engineers. Some jobs need ARM runners, and the firmware work needs a hardware-in-the-loop lab. We are comparing GitHub Actions, CircleCI and Buildkite.

# Comparison

table = Provider,Median build time (min),ARM runners,Self-hosted agents,Monthly cost; GitHub Actions,14.2,yes,yes,"$2,860"; CircleCI,11.8,yes,yes,"$3,420"; Buildkite,9.6,via our own agents,yes (required),"$2,150 plus our compute"

# Benchmarks

data bench = pipeline,GitHub Actions,CircleCI,Buildkite; firmware-build,21.4,17.9,13.2; cloud-api-tests,9.8,8.1,7.4; docs-site,3.1,2.7,2.9; sim-regression,34.5,29.0,22.6

chart bar bench y=GitHub Actions,CircleCI,Buildkite unit=min title="Median build time per reference pipeline"

# Pros and cons

### GitHub Actions

- Pros: ARM runners and self-hosted agents, lowest setup friction for our repos
- Cons: slowest median build time of the three in our benchmarks

### CircleCI

- Pros: ARM runners and self-hosted agents, faster than GitHub Actions on every pipeline
- Cons: highest monthly cost

### Buildkite

- Pros: fastest on every pipeline, self-hosted agents for the hardware lab, lowest license cost
- Cons: we run the agents and their compute ourselves, and self-hosted agents are required

# Recommendation

! Buildkite. The hardware-in-the-loop lab needs self-hosted agents anyway, so the extra operating work is already part of the plan.

!! Buildkite moves runner patching onto the DevEx on-call. We estimate this at 6 hours a month.
